<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Ile Royale</h1><p class="st-meta">1713 CE – 1763 CE · capital Louisbourg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">France's colony on Cape Breton Island, built around the fortress town of Louisbourg to guard the approaches to the St Lawrence after 1713.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-utrecht">Treaty of Utrecht</a> of 1713 gave <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> the peninsula of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/acadia">Acadia</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dominion-of-newfoundland">Newfoundland</a> but left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-cape-breton-island">Cape Breton</a> Island, renamed Ile Royale, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-edward-island">Ile Saint-Jean</a>, now <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-prince-edward-island">Prince Edward Island</a>. French settlers from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/plaisance">Plaisance</a> moved there, and from 1719 the Crown built the fortified town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisbourg">Louisbourg</a> on the east coast.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisbourg">Louisbourg</a>, with massive stone walls in the style of Vauban, was the colony's capital, garrison and port. The economy rested on the cod fishery, which made Ile Royale one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France's</a> most valuable colonies, and on trade with France, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies">West Indies</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-quebec">Quebec</a> and even <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a>. Governors such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-de-saint-ovide-de-brouillan">Joseph de Saint-Ovide de Brouillan</a> ran a society of soldiers, merchants and fishermen.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> forces captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisbourg">Louisbourg</a> in 1745, but it was returned in 1748. The British took it again in 1758, demolished the fortifications in 1760, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1763">Treaty of Paris</a> of 1763 ceded the island to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a>. Louisbourg was partly rebuilt in the 1960s.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ile-royale.jpg" alt="Ile Royale" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Sodacan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Royal_Standard_of_King_Louis_XIV.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1713 CE – 1763 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Louisbourg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Philippe Pastour de Costebelle, Joseph de Saint-Ovide de Brouillan, Augustin de Drucour</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Île Royale, French Cape Breton</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1738&amp;focus=ile-royale" aria-label="Ile Royale on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3262 560 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ile Royale lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3582 816L3327 789M3582 816L5054 728M3582 816L4997 651"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3327 789h0M5054 728h0M4997 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3582 816h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3582 816h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Louisbourg. <a href="/atlas?year=1738&amp;focus=ile-royale">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

